---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/apps/recart';
const title = 'Recart for Shopify: capture, SMS and sender continuity';
const desc = 'Evaluate Recart capture and SMS workflows, subscriber identity, replies, number migration and incremental contribution.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Shopify operations · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Recart for Shopify: capture, SMS and sender continuity</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Recart deserves evaluation as an SMS and list-growth platform, rather than a cart-only add-on. Its practical appeal is connecting a storefront signup to timely messaging with help operating the program. Start with the identity and permission path, then assess recovery and repeat-purchase economics. This review proposes acceptance exercises; it reports no merchant tests, recovery lifts or supplier ranking.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Current SMS scope and the legacy Messenger distinction</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://recart.com/">current Recart site</a> describes Shopify capture, OneClick opt-in, SMS campaigns and flows spanning welcome, cart, browse, replenishment and reviews. Its recognition-based OneClick experience is described for shoppers who previously used a Recart popup. That is broader than cart recovery; recognition still needs validation on the store’s actual devices and identities.</p>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/recart">Shopify listing</a> describes desktop/mobile popups, direct-to-text and email capture. A separate capture supplier is therefore not inherently required. Capture of an email address does not establish a full email sending suite; retain an existing email tool only where the chosen workflow needs it.</p>
      <p>Older <a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/blog/collecting-sms-subscribers-in-facebook-messenger">Recart partnership material</a> describes Messenger-based acquisition. That historical integration is not proof of a currently offered Messenger recovery program. The current product emphasis is SMS and capture. Ask for written account-specific confirmation before retaining any legacy Messenger flow, audience or cost assumption; do not compare hypothetical Messenger performance with SMS as if both channels were available today.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Prove capture and identity before counting subscribers</h2>
      <p>For a mobile beauty landing page, compare direct-to-text with the supported popup path. Inspect the distinction between opening a text composer, sending the opt-in text, recording a subscription and entering a welcome flow. On desktop, inspect the separate email and phone steps. Count completed eligible subscriptions rather than popup clicks or prefilled phone fields.</p>
      <p>Use controlled contacts for a fresh signup, returning recognized shopper, shared device, corrected phone number, existing email-only subscriber and withdrawn SMS contact. Record the normalized phone, email, source, offer and timestamped permission evidence. Check which system receives each field and how conflicting identities are resolved. Recognition must not silently attach another person’s phone or overwrite a withdrawal.</p>
      <p>Repeat a submission, retry a failed sync and complete a purchase between signup and welcome. Inspect whether the account issues duplicate discounts or replays historical events as fresh entries. Agree on the system of record for subscription state and the owner who investigates rejected records. These are proposed acceptance cases, not a claim that every rule can be automated in Recart.</p>
      <p>Unlike the <a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy review</a>, which also addresses supported account availability and Sendlane continuity, this evaluation centers on mobile identity, text replies and number continuity. Both products merit a usable-signup check; a larger raw capture count alone does not decide which store workflow works.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Four proposed messaging exercises</h2>
      <p><strong>Beauty flash sale:</strong> propose a $64 cart sequence with an initial reminder, useful product context and an optional capped 15% offer. The face discount is $9.60 and the resulting pre-tax price is $54.40. Check expiry, exclusions, stacking and margin before launch. Recheck purchase, stock and current permission at each dispatch; cancel obsolete reminders and deduplicate event retries.</p>
      <p><strong>Streetwear browse:</strong> propose three viewed products and a twenty-four-hour delay as candidate rules, not recommended universal thresholds. Verify usable browse events and product links in the actual account. A sold-out size, recent purchase or withdrawn subscription should change eligibility. Compare useful collection content with a reminder among comparable eligible shoppers, without assuming inventory-aware messaging is built in.</p>
      <p><strong>Home goods coordination:</strong> compare email-only with email plus SMS for shoppers eligible for both. Assign one owner to each recovery step and test cancellation across queues after purchase or withdrawal. An unopened email is neither reliable attention evidence nor SMS permission. Predefine the observation window and randomization unit; report contribution per assigned shopper and uncertainty rather than adding overlapping attributed revenues.</p>
      <p><strong>Reply and replenishment:</strong> propose a replenishment message for an eligible beauty buyer. Send controlled stock questions, a delivery problem and a withdrawal reply through the real receiving route. Identify who reads free-text replies, expected response coverage and escalation to support. Validate STOP/HELP behavior and suppression propagation before scaling; do not promise a staffed inbox or automated answer without account evidence.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Quote the whole workload and measure contribution</h2>
      <p>The <a class="link" href="https://recart.com/sms-marketing-pricing-ecommerce">current pricing page</a> separates list-growth and messaging configurations, with commitments, message allowances, carrier fees and number options. Obtain a dated quote for the selected configuration rather than reusing historical small-store bills. The <a class="link" href="https://recart.com/fully-managed-sms">managed-service page</a> describes onboarding, popup and campaign work; confirm the service entitlement and approval responsibilities in the actual proposal.</p>
      <p>Use two illustrative quote workloads: 1,800 SMS in an ordinary month and 6,500 SMS during a promotion. Specify destinations, SMS segments, MMS, failed sends, number rental, capture verification, migration, minimum commitment and operating labor. Message counts are inputs, not recipient counts or verified prices. Ask how unused allowances, overages and cancellation affect the total.</p>
      <p>For an illustrative experiment, suppose 40 incremental completed orders each yield $18 contribution after product costs, discounts and expected returns. That is $720 before messaging and program costs. If the incremental all-in program cost is $500 for the same window, the remaining contribution is $220. These assumptions are not merchant results; use actual uncertainty and fully loaded costs before deciding whether to continue.</p>
      <p>Track cost per usable new subscriber separately from recovery contribution. Divide allocated capture cost by genuinely new permissioned identities successfully recorded, excluding duplicate, unreachable and withdrawn records. Report opt-outs, reply workload and late purchases beside revenue. No order-count or subscriber threshold universally justifies another platform.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mb-8">
      <h2>Number continuity and operating ownership</h2>
      <p>Before a migration, inventory numbers, registrations, subscription evidence, suppressions, active flows, reply routes and scheduled campaigns. Ask whether the existing number can transfer, who controls the request and what happens if transfer is delayed. Recart advertises shortcode migration and verified toll-free assignment in its managed offering; those are different paths, not a guarantee of zero downtime for a particular account.</p>
      <p>Run a controlled cutover with an accountable queue owner: verify outbound sender identity, inbound replies, suppression import and a purchase that cancels an outstanding reminder. Preserve a rollback plan and stop the old sender from resuming duplicate jobs. Exported phone numbers without permission evidence and withdrawals are not a complete migration package.</p>
      <p>Keep a weekly review of reply backlog, failed handoffs, opt-out changes, discount spend and duplicate sends. Confirm purpose, channel and destination requirements for the actual program; tooling alone does not establish compliance. Consult the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">cart recovery guide</a> and <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript review</a> for further decisions, without treating those linked pages as newly verified evidence.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
